Z||000000||MooCow||12-05-2000||11:14 PM||||I am just wondering if there is a good price on a clutch?  What all needs to be changed when putting a new one in.  I am not going to be changing it cause I don't have the knowledge nor tools to do it.  How long of a process is this going to be?<P>                               Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||12-06-2000||06:24 AM||||I bought an OEM Borg Warner clutch kit which inclided the disk, pressure plate, throwout bearing, and pilot bearing (not needed on my 85sev-6 muncie 4spd) for $109.00 at Ken's Auto in Birmingham, AL.  It went in nicely and works great on my rebuilt 2.8.<P>I spent a bunch more money on cleaning up stuff while I had the engine out.  This is a good time to check all the exhaust bolts and to port the intake and exhaust manifolds.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||12-06-2000||07:04 AM||||autozone has clutch's for the v-6, for less than $120!<P>if you want a performance clutch, you'll pay more.<P>also, carolles is right.  it is a good tome to fix a few other things.  i wish i had pulled my manifolds and fixed any leaks, as well as ported them.<P>now i will have to wait another year to do that.<P>goood luck!  and remember, it ain't all that hard to change the clutch... with the forum around!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||12-06-2000||08:52 AM||||National Automotive may have the best price (mail order). I'm luckey I suppose because it's local for me! NEW Borg clutch, pressure plate and throw-out bearing in kit. $113.56 total (no shipping) for a V-6, part #90189. Call 1-800-428-4300 to order.<BR>($108.15 + local tax)||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||12-06-2000||09:01 AM||||I think that is waht I need to be replaced on my car?  When I let Ed Parks drice it he said I had a nice little car besides the clutch going out.  He said he does this job for $600.  The reason I am asking cause I am selling either the Fiero for cash to give to Ed Parks or selling my Ranger and giving th cash to Ed Parks.  If I sell my ranger and my Fiero then I am buying the 84 Fiero.  I have to decide if I want to drive my Fiero or the 84.  I am just worried about my clutch after Ed said that.  So do I need to bleed the system?  If so how do I?  What can I look for to see if it is my clutch?<P>                             Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000005||PlasticWrapd||12-06-2000||09:31 AM||||Before you go and buy a "kit" to replace the clutch, try pricing the individual pieces.  You'll be surprised how much money you can save.  I've found that the seperate parts can save you as much as $40.  It costs $116 for a "kit" to fit my VW Rabbit diesel, but the seperate pieces only runs about $78.  Go figure...  <P>BTW, I would NOT recommend using remanufactured parts.  As luck would have it, the one you install would have a flaw and have to be replaced again in a few months.  Is it worth all the labor again?  Warranties cover only the parts, not the labor.  Think about it.<p>[This message has been edited by PlasticWrapd (edited 12-06-2000).]||||reg||1
